## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of multi-tenant isolation in cloud-native ML serving, per-tenant observability, and governance in AWS SageMaker.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant routing with PrivateLink for network isolation
- Per-tenant Feature Store namespaces for data isolation
- Canary deployments with automated rollback on drift or latency degradation
- Per-tenant monitoring, autoscaling, and governance via Model Registry

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: Route inference request to tenant-specific endpoint
function routeToTenant(req, tenantId) {
  const endpoint = tenantEndpoints.get(tenantId);
  if (!endpoint) throw new Error('Unknown tenant');

  const result = await invokeEndpoint(endpoint, req.payload);

  // Check latency and trigger rollback if needed
  if (result.latencyP99 > latencyThreshold) {
    triggerAutoRollback(tenantId);
    alertOnCallers(tenantId, 'Latency spike detected');
  }

  return result;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests experience building production ML pipelines in AWS with strict data locality, latency targets, and privacy. Assesses ability to combine PrivateLink, Lake Formation, Feature Store, canary deployment, drift monitoring, and cost governance.

## Key Concepts
- SageMaker PrivateLink
- Lake Formation data access
- Feature Store
- Endpoint traffic splitting (canary)
- Drift detection & retraining triggers
- DP privacy controls and encryption

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example drift score calculation (KS statistic placeholder)
function ksDrift(p, q) {
  let max = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < p.length; i++) {
    max = Math.max(max, Math.abs(p[i] - q[i]));
  }
  return max;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rollback if canary drift triggers fire?
- What metrics and thresholds would you alert on for latency, FP rate, and drift?
